BidYourOffer — third-party data attribution notices
===================================================

This file collects the attribution notices owed to upstream data providers whose
data this application redistributes to end users. It exists because two of those
licenses REQUIRE it: CDLA-Permissive-2.0 requires attribution to accompany the
data, and Apache-2.0 §4(d) requires that a readable copy of the attribution
notices from a redistributed work's own NOTICE file travel with it.

A notice here is not documentation. It is a license obligation, and the surface
that discharges it for end users is the "Data Sources & Licenses" page
(route `data-sources`) together with the Location DNA attribution component
(`resources/views/partials/location-dna/_data-attribution.blade.php`). Editing
this file without updating `config/location_attribution.php` will fail
`tests/Unit/LocationDna/LocationDataAttributionTest.php`, which asserts the two
agree.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Overture Maps Foundation — Places theme
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Portions of the points-of-interest data in this application are derived from the
Overture Maps Foundation Places theme.

  https://overturemaps.org/
  https://docs.overturemaps.org/attribution/

THE PLACES THEME IS A MIXED-LICENSE AGGREGATE. It is NOT ODbL. Four of Overture's
six themes (Buildings, Transportation, Divisions, Base) are ODbL; Places is not
one of them, and treating it as ODbL is the common and incorrect assumption. The
Places theme combines contributions under three licenses:

  CDLA-Permissive-2.0   the bulk of the theme — Meta, Microsoft, PinMeTo, Krick,
                        RenderSEO, DAC, BrightQuery.
                        ATTRIBUTION REQUIRED.
                        https://cdla.dev/permissive-2-0/

  Apache-2.0            the Foursquare Open Source Places slice.
                        ATTRIBUTION REQUIRED, AND the upstream NOTICE file must
                        be reproduced — see the section below.
                        https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

  CC0-1.0               the AllThePlaces slice.
                        No requirements.
                        https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/

WE CANNOT TELL WHICH ROW CAME FROM WHICH MEMBER. The raw Overture record carries
`sources[].dataset`, but the import normalizer counts those datasets and discards
their names: the corpus retains `source_count` (an integer) and `source = 'overture'`
only. Verified against the loaded corpus — 29,434 rows, zero carrying per-source
attribution. Per-row license precision is therefore a corpus change (teaching the
importer to retain `sources[].dataset` and re-importing), not a config change.

The consequence for attribution is the important part: because any given row MAY
be the Foursquare slice, EVERY published row must be treated as though it is. The
strictest obligation in the aggregate — the Apache-2.0 NOTICE requirement — governs
the whole theme as we hold it. Naming CDLA alone would be false for the Foursquare
and AllThePlaces rows and would silently drop the one obligation here that could
actually be breached.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Foursquare Open Source Places — Apache-2.0 NOTICE and change notice
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Apache-2.0 §4(d) obliges us to reproduce the attribution notices contained in the
NOTICE file of the work we redistribute, and the Foursquare NOTICE itself states
the same three obligations in its own words: provide recipients with a copy of the
License, include prominent notices to the extent the Data has been changed, and
preserve attribution to Foursquare including the full content of that NOTICE.

All three are satisfied by files in this repository:

  resources/legal/foursquare-os-places-NOTICE.txt
      Foursquare's NOTICE, VERBATIM AND UNEDITED. Retrieved 2026-09-09 from
      https://opensource.foursquare.com/places-notice-txt/ — the URL Overture's
      own attribution page links as the authoritative source. Foursquare publishes
      it as a web page rather than a downloadable .txt, so the committed file is
      that page's notice content with HTML markup removed and nothing else changed.

  resources/legal/apache-2.0-LICENSE.txt
      The Apache License, Version 2.0, in full, as published at
      https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt

  resources/legal/overture-corpus-MODIFICATIONS.txt
      OUR notice of changes. Application-authored, and deliberately NOT merged into
      Foursquare's NOTICE: that NOTICE permits appending a change notice to it, but
      a merged file leaves a reader unable to tell whose sentences are whose, and
      preserving the NOTICE as Foursquare's is the point of the obligation.

The Foursquare copyright is reproduced in the committed NOTICE and is carried into
the user-visible attribution on /data-sources and beside any points of interest.

NOTE ON THE COPYRIGHT YEAR. Overture's attribution page quotes the Foursquare
credit as "Copyright 2024 Foursquare Labs, Inc. All rights reserved."; the NOTICE
that page links to states "© 2026 Foursquare Labs, Inc. All rights reserved." The
committed file preserves what the NOTICE itself says, because that is the artifact
whose full content we are required to preserve.

